DO NOT CREATE A SECOND JOINT SYSTEM

Inspect the existing logical joint graph and constraint creation path.

Extend the existing authoritative joint model.

There must remain:

- One logical joint graph
- One joint-ID authority
- One physics-constraint derivation path
- One snapshot representation
- One undo/redo model
- One stability-test activation path

Do not create a parallel “universal binding” collection that can coexist or
conflict with existing joints.

UNIVERSAL BINDING MODEL

Introduce or formalize one generic logical connection:

CONSTRUCTION_BINDING

A Construction Binding contains:

- Binding ID
- Connection subtype
- Piece A ID
- Feature A ID
- Piece B ID
- Feature B ID
- Material A
- Material B
- Canonical material-pair key
- Shared seam or connection point
- Final transform references
- Rest position
- Rest angle
- Strength-profile ID
- Compliance profile
- Current state
- Creation reason
- Geometry schema version

Existing specialized joints may remain meaningful subtypes, including:

- TIMBER_SPLICE
- FACE_BINDING
- CORNER_BINDING
- BLOCK_BINDING
- ROOF_PEAK
- DIAGONAL_BRACE
- T_JOINT
- ENVIRONMENTAL_ANCHOR

The general Construction Binding provides common lifecycle and material-pair
behavior.

ROOF_PEAK and ENVIRONMENTAL_ANCHOR may retain specialized directional behavior.

GEOMETRY FIRST, MATERIAL SECOND

Connection eligibility should be evaluated in this order:

1. Are the two features geometrically compatible?
2. Can they be aligned within exact or forgiving tolerance?
3. Is final placement collision-valid?
4. Is the connection permitted by level boundaries and editing phase?
5. Which connection subtype best represents the geometry?
6. Which material-pair profile determines physical strength and presentation?

Do not reject a valid connection merely because one piece is timber and the
other is stone.

Do not infer a joint from arbitrary overlap.

UNIVERSAL JOINT-CAPABLE FEATURES

Treat these geometric relationships as potentially bindable:

- Endpoint to endpoint
- Endpoint to compatible face
- Endpoint to corner
- Corner to corner
- Compatible face to face
- Designated brace socket
- Designated T-joint socket
- Roof Peak relationship
- Environmental anchor socket

Every current and future construction piece should expose authoritative geometric
features through the centralized piece definition.

Player-placeable and prebuilt pieces of the same geometry family should expose
the same ordinary faces and corners.

Prebuilt pieces may additionally expose environmental-anchor sockets.

Do not let legacy prebuilt sockets suppress ordinary geometric candidates.

PREBUILT PIECE COMPATIBILITY

Prebuilt stone/environment blocks must expose:

- Top face
- Bottom face
- Left face
- Right face
- Four corners
- Face centers
- Relevant joint-capable features

These features must use the same world-transform calculations as player pieces.

Prebuilt status may affect:

- Whether the prebuilt piece can move
- Binding strength
- Whether an environmental anchor profile applies
- Undo/edit ownership

Prebuilt status must not prevent an ordinary compatible piece from binding to a
valid face or corner.

TIMBER-TO-STONE BINDING

When a Timber Beam endpoint intentionally snaps to a stone face or corner:

- Align it flush.
- Preserve the player-selected angle.
- Avoid penetration.
- Create a breakable mixed-material binding.
- Use a clearly visible provisional clamp, rune, collar, or binding.
- Initialize stress-free rest geometry.
- Allow ordinary timber weight and basic roof load.
- Permit breakage under sufficient tension, shear, torque, support movement, or
  projectile impact.

Do not require a special inventory item for the prototype.

This binding is a construction convention of the alchemical world, not literal
real-world carpentry.

WOOD-BLOCK-TO-STONE BINDING

When a Wood Block intentionally snaps face-to-face or corner-to-corner with a
Stone Block:

- Permit a breakable mixed-material binding.
- Preserve geometry and collider validity.
- Do not require a hidden anchor.
- Do not convert the two pieces into one rigid body.

This is particularly important when:

- A wooden wall block sits on a stone foundation.
- A timber-compatible block extends a prebuilt stone wall.
- A roof support uses mixed materials.

STONE-TO-STONE BINDING

When two stone blocks intentionally snap through compatible faces:

- Create a breakable stone masonry-style binding.
- Allow stable walls and columns under ordinary gravity.
- Preserve separate physics bodies.
- Allow cracks, shear failure, separation, and collapse under force.
- Do not make stacked stone permanently rigid.
- Do not create a binding for free placement with Snap disabled unless the player
  explicitly selects a bind action.

A visually aligned stone wall should no longer consist only of unrelated loose
blocks when the player intentionally uses Snap to assemble it.

WOOD-TO-WOOD BINDING

Preserve accepted wood-to-wood behavior.

Migrate it to the universal lifecycle where appropriate without changing its
accepted results.

Wood bindings should remain:

- More compliant
- More flexible
- More vulnerable to bending and shear
- Capable of limited flex before breakage

MATERIAL-PAIR PROFILES

Centralize material-pair profiles.

At minimum:

- WOOD_WOOD
- WOOD_STONE
- STONE_STONE
- WOOD_ENVIRONMENT
- STONE_ENVIRONMENT
- ROOF_PEAK_WOOD
- ENVIRONMENTAL_ANCHOR

Each profile may define:

- Tensile strength
- Shear strength
- Bending strength
- Torque limit
- Linear compliance
- Angular compliance
- Damping
- Strain threshold
- Break threshold
- Presentation style

Use canonical pair ordering so:

- WOOD_STONE
- STONE_WOOD

resolve to the same profile unless direction is explicitly meaningful.

Do not maintain duplicated mirrored profiles.

PROVISIONAL RELATIVE BEHAVIOR

Use measured ordinary-load forces and existing material behavior to select
values.

Expected relative behavior:

WOOD_WOOD

- Flexible
- Moderate tension
- Lower shear and bending resistance
- Visible flex

WOOD_STONE

- Less flexible at the stone interface
- Moderate ordinary-load stability
- Vulnerable to torque and pullout
- Strong enough for basic rafters on stone walls
- Weaker than a true environmental anchor

STONE_STONE

- Strong under compression
- Moderate shear resistance
- Low flexibility
- Vulnerable to separation, lateral shear, and foundation movement
- Not indestructible

ENVIRONMENTAL_ANCHOR

- Stronger than ordinary construction bindings
- Uses existing accepted anchor behavior

Do not tune bindings to make every structure survive.

STRESS-FREE COMMIT

Every committed Construction Binding must derive rest geometry after final
transforms are validated.

Required:

- Rest connection point equals the final shared seam or feature relationship.
- Rest relative position matches committed geometry.
- Rest relative angle matches committed geometry.
- Initial positional error is near zero.
- Initial angular error is near zero.
- Initial stored constraint energy is near zero.

Do not initialize constraints from:

- Candidate-preview coordinates
- Pre-correction transforms
- Stale socket positions
- Previous placement order
- Legacy center sockets when a corner connection was committed

STABILITY ACTIVATION

Before the first physics step:

1. Instantiate all accepted bodies at their final committed transforms.
2. Clear stale linear and angular velocities.
3. Instantiate all Construction Bindings exactly once.
4. Instantiate Roof Peak and anchor specialization.
5. Validate constraint count against logical binding count.
6. Confirm no initial collider penetration.
7. Record initial constraint errors.
8. Activate gravity only after the complete binding graph exists.

Do not let one connection load the structure before the other bindings are
created.

ORDINARY-GRAVITY CONTRACT

A modest, visually reasonable construction assembled with committed bindings
should not immediately disintegrate under the ordinary weight of its own
components.

Examples:

- A two- or three-block stone wall should remain together under ordinary gravity.
- A wooden block bound to a stone foundation should remain in place under its
  own weight.
- A timber rafter bound to a stone wall should remain attached under ordinary
  timber load.
- A peaked roof with two lower bindings and one Roof Peak should remain
  assembled if its supports remain upright.
- A post-and-beam frame with valid bindings should remain assembled under
  ordinary gravity.

This does not guarantee stability when:

- Supports slide or tip
- Foundations are narrow
- A long span lacks support
- Excessive mass is added
- One connection is missing
- The structure is visibly lopsided
- Joints are already damaged
- A projectile strikes
- Debris impacts the structure

VISUAL CONNECTION LANGUAGE

Every committed binding should have a small visible construction treatment.

Examples:

WOOD_WOOD

- Timber collar
- Binding wrap
- Wooden joinery mark

WOOD_STONE

- Alchemical clamp
- Glowing rune band
- Bracket or magical mortar seam

STONE_STONE

- Mortar seam
- Alchemical masonry glyph
- Small edge binding

ROOF_PEAK

- Peak cap or centered binding

These visuals are provisional.

Requirements:

- Presentation-only
- No physical body
- No collider
- Hidden during candidate-only state
- Visible after commit
- Removed or broken visually when the binding fails
- Distinct from Snap sockets and support markers

CONNECTION-STATE CLARITY

Preserve clear states:

CANDIDATE

- Dashed/pulsing
- Not committed

ALIGNED

- Geometrically aligned only
- No binding

SUPPORTED

- Contact support only
- No binding

BOUND

- Solid binding marker
- Logical binding exists

STRAINED

- Binding exists and is under meaningful stress

BROKEN

- Constraint removed
- Broken presentation

The player should be able to tell before stability testing whether each junction
is actually bound.

SNAP ENABLED VERSUS DISABLED

SNAP ENABLED

- Compatible near features may create a binding candidate.
- Valid release commits the binding.
- Small forgiving correction may occur.

SNAP DISABLED

- Free placement remains available.
- Ordinary contact remains possible.
- Do not automatically create a structural binding merely because two pieces
  appear close.
- If useful, offer a contextual “Bind” action when compatible features are
  already within binding tolerance.

This preserves freeform placement while giving Snap a meaningful construction
purpose.

UNIVERSAL BIND ACTION

For pieces manually placed with Snap disabled, provide an optional contextual:

“Bind Connection”

Show this only when:

- Exactly one or a small number of compatible relationships are within binding
  tolerance.
- Final geometry is valid.
- No overlap exists.

If several relationships are plausible:

- Show candidate cycling.
- Do not bind randomly.

Binding is:

- Atomic
- Undoable
- Visibly previewed
- Governed by the same universal model

Do not require Bind Connection during ordinary Snap-enabled building.

CANDIDATE RANKING

When several connection candidates exist, prioritize:

1. Valid geometry
2. Player-selected angle preservation
3. Near feature compatibility
4. Architectural intent
5. Smallest correction
6. Candidate hysteresis
7. Stable deterministic tie-breaker

Examples:

- Angled beam near stone upper corner:
  prefer a corner binding over a distant face-center candidate.

- Wood block on top of stone foundation:
  prefer face-to-face foundation binding.

- Stone block against stone block:
  prefer face-to-face masonry binding.

- Opposed rafters:
  prefer Roof Peak.

Do not allow legacy prebuilt sockets to outrank a clearly closer modular corner
unless an explicit environmental anchor is intentionally selected.

PREBUILT PIECE RULES

Prebuilt pieces may be:

- Fixed
- Movable
- Environment-owned
- Player-owned

Fixed prebuilt pieces:

- Do not move during joint correction.
- May receive universal bindings.
- May expose stronger environmental-anchor candidates separately.

Movable prebuilt pieces:

- Follow ordinary edit rules.

Bindings to fixed prebuilt pieces:

- Apply correction only to the active player piece.
- Do not move fixed geometry.
- Remain breakable unless an environmental anchor is explicitly selected.

UNSUPPORTED AND WEAK STRUCTURES

Universal bindings must not remove structural strategy.

Examples:

- A roof on two loose blocks may push the blocks outward and collapse.
- A tall narrow stone wall may tip as a bound assembly.
- A long bound timber span may sag or break.
- A bound wall on a weak foundation may fall.
- A projectile may break the binding rather than the pieces.
- A joint may remain intact while a piece itself cracks or breaks.

The game should distinguish:

- Binding failure
- Piece failure
- Support movement
- Foundation failure
- Missing connection

SUPPORT AND LOAD DIAGNOSTICS

Before stability testing, optionally communicate:

- All intended junctions bound
- One junction supported only
- One junction unresolved
- Loose foundation
- Narrow support may tip
- Roof complete, foundations unanchored

Do not claim guaranteed stability.

Player-facing messages must remain concise.

UNDO, REDO, AND EDITING

Universal bindings must integrate with automatic joint-edit transactions.

Moving or rotating a bound piece:

- Begins binding-edit preview.
- Temporarily suspends affected bindings in the preview layer.
- Preserves neighboring pieces.
- Previews new relationships.
- Commits the final binding graph atomically.
- Cancel restores exact prior transforms and bindings.

Undo and redo must restore:

- Piece transforms
- Binding IDs
- Material-pair profiles
- Binding states
- Construction revision

No duplicate constraints.

REINFORCEMENT

Use universal bindings during reinforcement.

Requirements:

- Intact pieces may form new bindings.
- Broken debris cannot bind.
- Broken bindings remain broken unless intact pieces form a new deliberate
  connection.
- Surviving wisps block invalid overlap.
- Bindings remain breakable during Attack 2.
- Reinforcement history remains isolated.

