ACCEPTANCE SCENARIOS

A. TIMBER TO STONE

- Place an angled timber endpoint near a stone upper corner.
- Confirm a universal mixed-material binding candidate.
- Commit.
- Confirm a solid binding marker.
- Test stability.
- Confirm ordinary timber weight does not immediately separate it.

B. TIMBER TO PREBUILT STONE

- Repeat with a fixed blue-gray prebuilt block.
- Confirm ordinary modular corner geometry is available.
- Confirm the prebuilt piece does not move.
- Confirm the binding remains breakable.

C. WOOD BLOCK ON STONE FOUNDATION

- Snap a Wood Block onto a Stone Block face.
- Confirm a committed mixed-material binding.
- Test stability.
- Confirm the wood block remains attached under its own weight.

D. STONE WALL

- Bind two or three stone blocks.
- Confirm a visible masonry-style binding.
- Confirm ordinary-gravity stability.
- Apply lateral force or projectile impact.
- Confirm possible shear, tipping, cracking, or binding failure.

E. PEAKED ROOF ON STONE SUPPORTS

- Use two stone or prebuilt supports.
- Bind lower rafter endpoints to the inner upper corners.
- Bind rafters at Roof Peak.
- Confirm all three committed relationships.
- Test stability.
- Confirm roof remains assembled if supports remain stable.

F. PEAKED ROOF ON LOOSE SUPPORTS

- Use loose, narrow support blocks.
- Confirm all bindings exist.
- Test stability.
- Permit supports to slide or tip naturally.
- Confirm failure is attributed to foundation movement, not missing bindings.

G. MIXED FRAME

- Create a structure using wood beams, a wood block, stone blocks, and a
  prebuilt piece.
- Confirm predictable connectivity across materials.
- Confirm different bindings use appropriate profiles.

H. SNAP OFF

- Place compatible pieces manually with Snap disabled.
- Confirm no automatic binding.
- Use Bind Connection.
- Confirm one valid binding is committed.

I. BREAKAGE

- Strike each binding class with a suitable projectile or diagnostic impulse.
- Confirm bindings can strain and break.
- Confirm pieces remain separate authoritative bodies.

J. EDITING

- Move a universally bound piece without manual detachment.
- Cancel and confirm exact restoration.
- Commit elsewhere and confirm an atomic graph replacement.

K. REPEATED TESTING

- Test and restore mixed-material construction three times.
- Confirm binding and constraint counts remain constant.
- Confirm no accumulated energy.

L. REINFORCEMENT

- Create mixed-material bindings during reinforcement.
- Confirm wisp clearance and history rules.

M. PERSISTENCE

- Reload, Retry Design, Start Over, and Reset Level.
- Confirm bindings restore appropriately.
- Confirm no legacy duplicates.

N. REGRESSION

Run all existing suites and production build.

